  "statement": "The lines $y=2x+1$ and $y=-x+4$ meet at $A$. Find the coordinates of $A$, and hence find the area of triangle $ABC$ with $B(0,1)$ and $C(4,0)$.",
  "solution": "Intersection: $2x+1=-x+4\\Rightarrow 3x=3\\Rightarrow x=1$, so $y=3$; thus $A(1,3)$. Area of triangle with vertices $(x_{1},y_{1}),(x_{2},y_{2}),(x_{3},y_{3})$ is $\\tfrac12|x_{1}(y_{2}-y_{3})+x_{2}(y_{3}-y_{1})+x_{3}(y_{1}-y_{2})|$. Here $=\\tfrac12|1(1-0)+0(0-3)+4(3-1)|=\\tfrac12|1+0+8|=\\tfrac{9}{2}$.",
  "hints": [
    "Equate the two line equations to find $A$.",
    "Apply the shoelace formula for the area."
  ],
